{"description":"Jane Fonda playfully claims she should have delivered the Oscars tribute to the late Robert Redford, comparing her four‑film collaborations with him to Barbra Streisand’s single film partnership.","articleSummary":"During the 98th Academy Awards, Jane Fonda joked to Entertainment Tonight that she ought to have spoken at the In Memoriam segment honoring the late Robert Redford, citing that she starred in four movies with him while Barbra Streisand appeared in only one.  Streisand performed a portion of “The Way We Were” and praised Redford’s courage and influence on independent cinema.  Fonda reflected on their joint work, noting their collaborations in *The Chase* (1966), *Barefoot in the Park* (1967), *The Electric Horseman* (1979), and *Our Souls at Night* (2017), and expressed affection for Redford’s personality and values.

Redford died in September 2025 at age 89.  Both Fonda and Streisand posted tributes on Instagram shortly after his death, highlighting his impact on filmmaking and his “intellectual cowboy” persona.  The article situates the Oscars tribute within the broader context of the ceremony, mentioning host Conan O’Brien and the Dolby Theatre venue.

